Project-specific context for this phase:

Two codelines are in scope, both pure TypeScript modules with vitest + typescript
and no external runtime dependencies:

- mocka (root: /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a) — src/fares.ts exports
  `fareFor(rider: Rider)` and `totalFor(riders: Rider[])` where
  `Rider = { age: number; hasPass: boolean }`. Fare constants are
  `BASE_FARE_CENTS` (350) and `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` (175). Ticket MOCK3-1 is a
  boundary bug: riders aged exactly 65 must receive the concession fare, so the
  age comparison is the critical property to fuzz. Input domain for `age` is a
  non-negative integer; boundary values of interest are 12, 13, 64, 65, 66.
  `hasPass` is a boolean that short-circuits to 0. For `totalFor`, the input
  domain is arrays of `Rider` (including empty array and single-element array).

- mockb (root: /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b) — src/schedule.ts
  exports `formatStops(stops: Stop[])` and `totalMinutes(stops: Stop[])` where
  `Stop = { name: string; minutes: number }`. Ticket MOCK3-2 is an off-by-one in
  the rendering loop: the last stop is omitted. Input domain for `formatStops` is
  arrays of `Stop` (including empty array, single-element array, and arrays of
  length ≥ 2); the invariant to check is that every stop in the input appears in
  the output string. For `totalMinutes`, the invariant is that the result equals
  the `minutes` field of the last element (or 0 for an empty array).

Both codelines use vitest. When writing an executableTest skeleton, use vitest
idioms: `import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest'` and import the function
under test from the relative `../src/` path, matching the existing test files.

